1. What Is the Java Collections Framework?

    The Java Collections Framework (JCF) is a unified architecture provided by Java to store, retrieve, manipulate, and process groups of objects.

    Instead of manually managing arrays, Java provides ready-made data structures such as:

        ArrayList
        LinkedList
        HashSet
        TreeSet
        HashMap
        TreeMap
        PriorityQueue
        Deque

    Without Collections:

        String[] names = new String[3];

        names[0] = "Alice";
        names[1] = "Bob";
        names[2] = "Charlie";

    Problems:

        Fixed size
        Difficult insertion/deletion
        No built-in searching or sorting flexibility

    Using Collections:

        ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

        names.add("Alice");
        names.add("Bob");
        names.add("Charlie");

        System.out.println(names);

        Output:

        [Alice, Bob, Charlie]

    Q: Why do we need Collections when arrays already exist?

        Arrays are useful when:
            Size is fixed
            Performance and direct indexing are important
            We know the number of elements

    Collections are useful when:

        Size changes dynamically
        We need insertion/deletion/search utilities
        We need uniqueness, key-value storage, sorting, queues, etc.

2. Collection vs Collections vs Collection Framework

    Collection

        Collection is an interface.

        public interface Collection<E>

        It represents a group of objects.

        Examples of interfaces:

        List
        Set
        Queue

    Collections

        Collections is a utility class.

        java.util.Collections

        It contains static methods for operating on collections.

            Collections.sort(list);
            Collections.reverse(list);
            Collections.shuffle(list);
            Collections.max(list);
            Collections.min(list);

    Example:

        ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

        numbers.add(30);
        numbers.add(10);
        numbers.add(20);

        Collections.sort(numbers);

        System.out.println(numbers);

        Output:

        [10, 20, 30]
        Collections Framework

    This refers to the entire architecture:

        Interfaces
        Implementations
        Algorithms
        Utility methods
        Iterators
        Important distinction
        Term	Meaning
        Collection	Interface
        Collections	Utility class
        Collection Framework	Complete architecture
        Student doubt

    Q: Is Collections a parent class of ArrayList = No.

        Collections → Utility class
        ArrayList → Implementation class

        They are unrelated through inheritance.


    Iterable
       |
    Collection
       |
       |---------------- List
       |                  |
       |                  |--- ArrayList
       |                  |--- LinkedList
       |                  |--- Vector
       |                  |--- Stack
       |
       |---------------- Set
       |                  |
       |                  |--- HashSet
       |                  |--- LinkedHashSet
       |                  |--- SortedSet
       |                         |
       |                         |--- NavigableSet
       |                                |
       |                                |--- TreeSet
       |
       |---------------- Queue
                          |
                          |--- PriorityQueue
                          |
                          |--- Deque
                               |
                               |--- ArrayDeque
                               |--- LinkedList

    Map is separate
    
    Map
     |
     |--- HashMap
     |--- LinkedHashMap
     |--- SortedMap
          |
          |--- NavigableMap
                 |
                 |--- TreeMap
     |
     |--- Hashtable

    Map does not extend Collection.


    Q: Why is Map not part of Collection?

    Because a Collection stores individual elements:

    [A, B, C]

    A Map stores key-value pairs:

    101 → "Alice"
    102 → "Bob"

    The data model is fundamentally different.